  Context: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a stub — [38;5;153mlogin(username, password)[39m just [38;5;153mconsole.log[39ms and returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m.
  The only caller is the form submit handler, which reads [38;5;153musername[39m and [38;5;153mpassword[39m from the DOM. [38;5;153msrc/[39m is unrelated (a
  [38;5;153mgreet[39m demo).

  One thing blocks me before I can write anything.

  [1mThe fork:[0m the login form only has [38;5;153musername[39m and [38;5;153mpassword[39m, so at the moment [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is called there is no user ID in
  hand — the thing that assigns a user ID is the authentication that hasn't happened yet. So "userId parameter" can mean
  three different things, and they differ in what the caller has to produce and what the function signature commits to.

  - [1mClient-generated correlation ID[0m — caller mints an ID (e.g. [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m) per login attempt and passes it in.
    Buys you traceable attempts including failures; costs a new ID-generation call site. Doesn't identify the human — it
    identifies the attempt. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mCaller-supplied account ID[0m — signature becomes [38;5;153mlogin(userId, username, password)[39m and the form must acquire a real
    account ID from somewhere. Buys a true identity; costs a source that doesn't exist yet (new form field, or a lookup
    call before login). This is the expensive one and it leaks into the HTML.
  - [1mServer-assigned ID, returned not passed[0m — [38;5;153mlogin[39m keeps its signature and the returned object gains [38;5;153muserId[39m. Buys
    correct semantics (IDs come from auth); costs: it is not the parameter you asked for. Changing this later means
    changing the return contract, which is the one thing callers depend on.

  [1mMy recommendation: the correlation ID.[0m It's the only one of the three that actually works with the data available at
  call time, it gives you "who attempted to log in, and did it succeed" in the log line, and it doesn't require
  inventing an identity source in a stub that doesn't authenticate yet.
